The modulus is the <Euclidean norm>, obtained by taking the square root of the <dot product> of the <vector> with itself:
$$
\boxed{\sqrt{1^2+2^2+1^2}=\sqrt6.}
$$

= b
{parent=1}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=b}

The <dot product> is $1\cdot0+2(-1)+(-1)\cdot1=-3$, while the <Euclidean norms> are $\sqrt6$ and $\sqrt2$. Therefore the <angle> $\theta\in[0,\pi]$ satisfies
$$
\cos\theta=\frac{-3}{\sqrt6\sqrt2}=-\frac{\sqrt3}{2},\qquad \boxed{\theta=\frac{5\pi}{6}=150^\circ.}
$$

= 2
{parent=Paper 1}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=2}

The <derivative> is $y'=2x+1/x$, so the <tangent line> has <slope> $3$ at $x=1$. The corresponding <point> is $(1,1)$ because $\ln1=0$. Hence the <tangent line> is $y-1=3(x-1)$, or $y=3x-2$. Thus
$$
\boxed{\text{slope}=3,\qquad y\text{-intercept}=-2.}
$$

= 3
{parent=Paper 1}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=3}

The <chain rule> gives
$$
\boxed{f'(x)=-2x\exp(2-x^2).}
$$
Since $x^2\geq0$, the exponent is at most $2$, with equality precisely at $x=0$. The <exponential function> is increasing, so
$$
\boxed{\max_{x\in\mathbb R}f(x)=e^2,\qquad\text{attained at }x=0.}
$$
The sign of the <derivative>, positive for $x<0$ and negative for $x>0$, gives the same conclusion.

