= Solution
{parent=a}

Substitution for $y$ reduces the two <simultaneous equations> to
$$
x^2+(x-1)^4-1=x(x-1)(x^2-3x+4)=0.
$$
The remaining quadratic factor is positive for all real $x$, since $x^2-3x+4=(x-3/2)^2+7/4$. Thus there are exactly $\boxed{2\text{ real solutions}}$.

= b
{parent=6}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=b}

From the factorisation in part (a), $x=0$ or $x=1$. Substitution into the <parabola> equation gives respectively $y=1$ or $y=0$. Thus the two intersections of the <parabola> and the <unit circle> are
$$
\boxed{(x,y)=(0,1)\text{ and }(1,0).}
$$

= 7
{parent=Paper 1}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=7}

Since $\cos x>0$ throughout the given <interval>, multiplying by $\cos x$ is reversible. The equation simplifies to
$$
2-\sin x=\sqrt3\cos x,\qquad \sin x+\sqrt3\cos x=2\sin(x+\pi/3)=2.
$$
The <sine> equals $1$ only at $\pi/2+2j\pi$. Here $x+\pi/3\in[\pi/3,5\pi/6)$, so only $x+\pi/3=\pi/2$ is allowed. Hence
$$
\boxed{x=\pi/6.}
$$

= 8
{parent=Paper 1}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=8}

Divide by four and use <completing the square>:
$$
x^2-2x+y^2+y=\frac{11}{4},\qquad (x-1)^2+\left(y+\frac12\right)^2=4.
$$
This is the standard equation of a <circle>, so
$$
\boxed{\text{centre}=(1,-1/2),\qquad\text{radius}=2.}
$$

= 9
{parent=Paper 1}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=9}

The <binomial series> begins
$$
(1+ax)^n=1+nax+\frac{n(n-1)}2a^2x^2+O(x^3).
$$
Equating coefficients gives $na=-2$ and $n(n-1)a^2/2=3$. Since $na\ne0$, substitute $a=-2/n$ into the second equation to obtain $2(n-1)/n=3$, so $n=-2$ and $a=1$. Therefore
$$
\boxed{a=1,\qquad n=-2.}
$$
Indeed $(1+x)^{-2}=1-2x+3x^2-\cdots$ for $|x|<1$.

