{parent=i}

Write $z_1=a+ib$ and $z_2=c+id$ with real $a,b,c,d$. By the definition of <complex conjugation>,
$$
(z_1+z_2)^*=[(a+c)+i(b+d)]^*=(a+c)-i(b+d)=(a-ib)+(c-id)=\boxed{z_1^*+z_2^*.}
$$

= ii
{parent=a}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}

With $z_1=a+ib$ and $z_2=c+id$, direct multiplication gives $z_1z_2=(ac-bd)+i(ad+bc)$. Taking the <complex conjugate> yields
$$
(z_1z_2)^*=(ac-bd)-i(ad+bc)=(a-ib)(c-id)=\boxed{z_1^*z_2^*.}
$$

= b
{parent=11}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=b}

The given <complex numbers> have positive <real parts>, so $\arg z_1=\arctan A$ and $\arg z_2=\arctan B$. Their product is
$$
z_1z_2=(1-AB)+i(A+B).
$$
By the assumed <complex argument> ranges, this product also has positive <real part>, hence $1-AB>0$. Its <complex argument> is consequently $\arctan[(A+B)/(1-AB)]$, with no extra multiple of $\pi$. Since multiplication adds <complex arguments>,
$$
\boxed{\arctan A+\arctan B=\arctan\left(\frac{A+B}{1-AB}\right).}
$$
This proves the <arctangent addition formula> under the stated branch restriction. For arbitrary real $A,B$, the principal <inverse tangent> formula can require adding or subtracting $\pi$ when $AB>1$.

= c
{parent=11}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=c}

A counterclockwise <rotation> through $\alpha$ about the origin in the <complex plane> sends $z_j$ to $e^{i\alpha}z_j$. By part (a), and because the <complex conjugate> of $e^{i\alpha}$ is $e^{-i\alpha}$,
$$
(e^{i\alpha}z_1)(e^{i\alpha}z_2)^*=e^{i\alpha}z_1e^{-i\alpha}z_2^*=\boxed{z_1z_2^*.}
$$
Thus $Q$ is invariant under the common <rotation>.

= d
{parent=11}
{scope}

= i
{parent=d}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=i}

Write $z_j=x_j+iy_j$. Multiplication gives
$$
Q=x_1x_2+y_1y_2+i(y_1x_2-x_1y_2).
$$
Therefore the <real part> is the <dot product> of the corresponding <vectors>:
$$
\boxed{\Re Q=x_1x_2+y_1y_2=\mathbf v_1\cdot\mathbf v_2.}
$$
For nonzero <vectors>, this is $|z_1||z_2|\cos(\arg z_1-\arg z_2)$, measuring their alignment.

= ii
{parent=d}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}

From the same multiplication, the <imaginary part> is
$$
\boxed{\Im Q=y_1x_2-x_1y_2=-(\mathbf v_1\times\mathbf v_2)_z.}
$$
Here the planar <vectors> are embedded in three dimensions with zero third coordinate. Its magnitude is the <area> of their <parallelogram>; its sign is positive when the oriented <angle> from $\mathbf v_2$ to $\mathbf v_1$ has positive <sine>. The reversed order matters: $\Im(z_1z_2^*)$ is the negative of the usual signed <area> for the order $(\mathbf v_1,\mathbf v_2)$.

= e
{parent=11}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=e}

Since $x=\cos y$ with real $y$, necessarily $-1\leq x\leq1$. Use <Euler's formula> and set $w=e^{iy}\ne0$. Then
$$
2x=w+w^{-1},\qquad w^2-2xw+1=0,
$$
so the <quadratic equation> gives $w=x\pm i\sqrt{1-x^2}$. Let $q=x+i\sqrt{1-x^2}$. It lies on the upper half of the <unit circle>, so $q=e^{i\theta}$ with $0\leq\theta\leq\pi$. Choose the <complex logarithm> value $\ln q=i\theta$, taking $\ln(-1)=i\pi$ at $x=-1$. The two roots are $e^{\pm i\theta}$, so $e^{iy}=e^{\pm i\theta}$ gives $y=\pm\theta+2n\pi$.

Because $i\ln q=-\theta$, this set of real solutions is exactly
$$
\boxed{y=\pm i\ln\left(x+i\sqrt{1-x^2}\right)+2n\pi,\qquad n\in\mathbb Z.}
$$
The formula includes $x=\pm1$: the two sign families then coincide after adjusting $n$. No real $y$ exists if $|x|>1$.

= f
{parent=11}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=f}

For $\theta\notin2\pi\mathbb Z$, use the <finite geometric series> with ratio $e^{i\theta}$:
$$
\sum_{n=0}^{N-1}e^{in\theta}=\frac{1-e^{iN\theta}}{1-e^{i\theta}}=e^{i(N-1)\theta/2}\frac{\sin(N\theta/2)}{\sin(\theta/2)}.
$$
The second equality follows by factoring $1-e^{it}=-2ie^{it/2}\sin(t/2)$ in numerator and denominator. Taking <real parts> proves the <finite trigonometric sum> identity
$$
\boxed{\sum_{n=0}^{N-1}\cos(n\theta)=\frac{\cos((N-1)\theta/2)\sin(N\theta/2)}{\sin(\theta/2)}.}
$$
When $\theta=2p\pi$, every <cosine> is $1$, so the <sum> is $\boxed{N}$. This also gives the continuous limiting value at the excluded arguments.

