= Solution
{parent=a}

Take the dumbbell axis to be the $z$ axis, with its midpoint at $z=0$. The right-hand <sphere> has centre $z=\alpha a$ and <radius> $R=\beta a$, while its junction with the <cylinder> is at $z=\gamma a$. Put $d=(\alpha-\gamma)a$, so $0<d<R$. The <Pythagorean theorem> in a meridional cross-section gives the handle <radius> $H$ from $H^2+d^2=R^2$. Thus
$$
\boxed{H=a\sqrt{\beta^2-(\alpha-\gamma)^2}.}
$$

= b
{parent=12}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=b}

Use <cylindrical coordinates> $(r,\phi,z)$ about the dumbbell axis. Their <Jacobian determinant> contributes the volume factor $r$, so the handle's <mass> is the <volume integral>
$$
M_h=\rho\int_{-\gamma a}^{\gamma a}\int_0^{2\pi}\int_0^H r\,dr\,d\phi\,dz=\rho(2\gamma a)(2\pi)\frac{H^2}{2}.
$$
Inserting the <radius> from part (a) yields
$$
\boxed{M_h=2\pi\rho\gamma a^3\left[\beta^2-(\alpha-\gamma)^2\right].}
$$

= c
{parent=12}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=c}

Centre <cylindrical coordinates> on the right-hand <sphere>, with the axial coordinate $s=z-\alpha a$ positive outwards. Using $R=\beta a$ and $d=(\alpha-\gamma)a$, the retained solid satisfies
$$
-d\leq s\leq R,\qquad0\leq r\leq\sqrt{R^2-s^2},\qquad0\leq\phi<2\pi.
$$
In particular, it contains more than a <hemisphere>; it is the small cap on the inward side that has been removed. The <volume integral> for its <mass>, including the <Jacobian determinant> $r$, is
$$
\begin{aligned}
M_e&=\rho\int_{-d}^{R}\int_0^{2\pi}\int_0^{\sqrt{R^2-s^2}}r\,dr\,d\phi\,ds\\
&=\pi\rho\int_{-d}^{R}(R^2-s^2)\,ds\\
&=\pi\rho\left(\frac{2R^3}{3}+R^2d-\frac{d^3}{3}\right).
\end{aligned}
$$
Thus each end piece has <mass>
$$
\boxed{M_e=\pi\rho a^3\left[\frac{2\beta^3}{3}+\beta^2(\alpha-\gamma)-\frac{(\alpha-\gamma)^3}{3}\right].}
$$
Equivalently this is the <volume of a spherical cap> with height $R+d$, multiplied by the uniform <mass density>.

\Image[paper-1-q12-integration-region.png]
{title=The retained end piece and the two radial ranges used in spherical integration}

= d
{parent=12}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=d}

Use <spherical coordinates> $(r,\theta,\phi)$ centred on the right-hand <sphere>, with polar axis pointing outwards along $s$. The <Jacobian determinant> is $r^2\sin\theta$, and the retained region is $r\leq R$ with $r\cos\theta\geq-d$.

For $0\leq r\leq d$, every polar <angle> $0\leq\theta\leq\pi$ is allowed. For $d<r\leq R$, the cut requires $0\leq\theta\leq\arccos(-d/r)$. Splitting the <volume integral> at $r=d$ therefore gives
$$
\begin{aligned}
M_e={}&\rho\int_0^{2\pi}\left[\int_0^d\int_0^\pi r^2\sin\theta\,d\theta\,dr+\int_d^R\int_0^{\arccos(-d/r)}r^2\sin\theta\,d\theta\,dr\right]d\phi\\
={}&2\pi\rho\left[2\int_0^d r^2\,dr+\int_d^R r^2\left(1+\frac dr\right)\,dr\right]\\
={}&2\pi\rho\left[\frac{2d^3}{3}+\frac{R^3-d^3}{3}+\frac d2(R^2-d^2)\right]\\
={}&\boxed{\pi\rho\left(\frac{2R^3}{3}+R^2d-\frac{d^3}{3}\right).}
\end{aligned}
$$
Substitution of $R=\beta a$ and $d=(\alpha-\gamma)a$ reproduces the <mass> in part (c). The radial split avoids integrating over a polar <angle> range independent of $r$, which would incorrectly include part of the removed <spherical cap>.

= e
{parent=12}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=e}

The handle and the two end pieces have disjoint interiors; their common boundary <circles> contribute no <volume>. Hence the total <mass> is $M=M_h+2M_e$. With $\delta=\alpha-\gamma$, the results above give
$$
\boxed{M=2\pi\rho a^3\left[\gamma(\beta^2-\delta^2)+\frac{2\beta^3}{3}+\beta^2\delta-\frac{\delta^3}{3}\right].}
$$
Equivalently, since $\gamma+\delta=\alpha$,
$$
\boxed{M=2\pi\rho a^3\left[\alpha\beta^2+\frac{2\beta^3}{3}-\gamma(\alpha-\gamma)^2-\frac{(\alpha-\gamma)^3}{3}\right].}
$$

