= Solution
{parent=a}

On an <interval> with $x\ne0$, the equation is a <separable differential equation>. For $y\ne0$,
$$
\frac{dy}{y^2}=\left(\frac4{x^2}-x^2\right)dx.
$$
Taking <antiderivatives> gives $-1/y=-4/x-x^3/3+C$. Renaming the constant, the explicit general nonzero solution is
$$
\boxed{y(x)=\frac1{4/x+x^3/3+C}.}
$$
It is defined on any <interval> avoiding $x=0$ and zeros of its denominator. The separation divided by $y^2$, so the additional solution $\boxed{y(x)\equiv0}$ must be included.

= i
{parent=a}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=i}

Using the general solution in part (a), the <initial condition> gives
$$
1=4+\frac13+C,\qquad C=-\frac{10}{3}.
$$
Therefore the solution of this <initial value problem> is
$$
\boxed{y(x)=\frac{3x}{x^4-10x+12}.}
$$
The formula is used on the <interval> containing $x=1$ where the original <ordinary differential equation> and the displayed expression are defined.

= ii
{parent=a}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}

The zero solution satisfies both the <ordinary differential equation> and the <initial condition>. The right-hand side is continuously differentiable in $y$ near $(x,y)=(2,0)$, so the <Picard-Lindelöf theorem> gives local uniqueness; equivalently the nonzero family in part (a) can never take the value zero at $x=2$. Thus
$$
\boxed{y(x)=0\quad\text{on }(0,\infty).}
$$
The restriction $x>0$ is the maximal <interval> containing $2$ on which the original <ordinary differential equation> is defined.

= b
{parent=13}
{scope}

= i
{parent=b}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=i}

Let $P=\mu(x)(xy-16x-x^3)$ and $Q=\mu(x)(16+x^2)$. An <exact differential form> must satisfy $P_y=Q_x$. These <partial derivatives> give
$$
x\mu=(16+x^2)\mu'+2x\mu,\qquad \frac{\mu'}\mu=-\frac{x}{16+x^2}.
$$
Taking <antiderivatives> produces $\ln|\mu|=-\tfrac12\ln(16+x^2)+C$. A convenient nonzero <integrating factor for a differential one-form> is therefore
$$
\boxed{\mu(x)=\frac1{\sqrt{16+x^2}}.}
$$
An explicit potential, which also proves global exactness, is
$$
F(x,y)=y\sqrt{16+x^2}-\frac13(16+x^2)^{3/2}.
$$
Indeed $F_x=P$ and $F_y=Q$ for the chosen <integrating factor>.

= ii
{parent=b}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}

Multiplying the <ordinary differential equation> by the nonzero <integrating factor> from part (i) makes its left-hand side $dF(x,y(x))/dx$. Hence the potential is constant:
$$
y\sqrt{16+x^2}-\frac13(16+x^2)^{3/2}=C.
$$
Since $16+x^2>0$ for every real $x$, this is equivalent to the explicit general solution
$$
\boxed{y(x)=\frac{16+x^2}{3}+\frac{C}{\sqrt{16+x^2}},\qquad C\in\mathbb R.}
$$
It is defined for all real $x$. There are no lost solutions, since the <integrating factor> and the coefficient of $y'$ are everywhere nonzero.

