= Solution
{parent=a}

The <Pythagorean theorem> gives $c^2=a^2+b^2$. Taking a <total differential> gives $2c\,dc=2a\,da+2b\,db$, so
$$
\boxed{\frac{dc}{c}=\frac{a\,da+b\,db}{a^2+b^2}=\frac{a^2}{a^2+b^2}\frac{da}{a}+\frac{b^2}{a^2+b^2}\frac{db}{b}.}
$$
The <area> of the <right triangle> is $A=ab/2$, so the <product rule> gives $dA=(b\,da+a\,db)/2$ and
$$
\boxed{\frac{dA}{A}=\frac{da}{a}+\frac{db}{b}.}
$$
The prescribed small changes therefore give $dA/A\approx0.01-0.02=-0.01$: **the area decreases by approximately $1\%$**. For comparison, retaining the product of the two finite percentage changes gives $(1.01)(0.98)-1=-0.0102$, an exact decrease of $1.02\%$; the <total differential> retains only the first-order terms.

= b
{parent=14}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=b}

Put $q=\varphi'(y-\beta z)$. Apply <implicit differentiation> with respect to $x$ and $y$, using the <chain rule>:
$$
1-\alpha z_x=-\beta qz_x,\qquad -\alpha z_y=q(1-\beta z_y).
$$
Consequently
$$
(\alpha-\beta q)z_x=1,\qquad (\alpha-\beta q)z_y=-q.
$$
At a <point> where the assumed <differentiable function> $z(x,y)$ exists, the first equation forces $\alpha-\beta q\ne0$. Combining the equations therefore gives
$$
\boxed{\alpha z_x+\beta z_y=\frac{\alpha-\beta q}{\alpha-\beta q}=1.}
$$
The assertion is understood on such differentiable branches of the <implicit function>; arbitrary parameter choices need not produce one everywhere.

= c
{parent=14}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=c}

The scaling relation says that $z$ is a <homogeneous function> of degree $n$. Fix $(x,y)$ and differentiate the identity with respect to the positive parameter $\lambda$. The <chain rule> gives
$$
xz_x(\lambda x,\lambda y)+yz_y(\lambda x,\lambda y)=n\lambda^{n-1}z(x,y).
$$
Setting $\lambda=1$ proves the <Euler theorem for homogeneous functions> identity
$$
\boxed{xz_x+yz_y=nz.}
$$
Differentiate once more with respect to $\lambda$, using equality of the mixed <partial derivatives> for the assumed twice continuously differentiable <function>:
$$
x^2z_{xx}(\lambda x,\lambda y)+2xyz_{xy}(\lambda x,\lambda y)+y^2z_{yy}(\lambda x,\lambda y)=n(n-1)\lambda^{n-2}z(x,y).
$$
At $\lambda=1$ this is the <second-order Euler identity for homogeneous functions>:
$$
\boxed{x^2z_{xx}+2xyz_{xy}+y^2z_{yy}=n(n-1)z.}
$$
The repeated parameter <differentiation> accounts for the factor $n(n-1)$, rather than $n^2$.

