{parent=c}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=i}

The <Maclaurin series> of the <hyperbolic cosine> is $\cosh x=1+x^2/2+x^4/24+O(x^6)$. The <binomial series> with exponent $-1/2$ gives $(1+u)^{-1/2}=1-u/2+3u^2/8+O(u^3)$. Substituting $u=x^2/2+x^4/24+O(x^6)$ yields
$$
\begin{aligned}
(\cosh x)^{-1/2}&=1-\frac12\left(\frac{x^2}{2}+\frac{x^4}{24}\right)+\frac38\left(\frac{x^2}{2}\right)^2+O(x^6)\\
&=\boxed{1-\frac{x^2}{4}+\frac{7x^4}{96}+O(x^6).}
\end{aligned}
$$
These are the first three nonzero terms; the remainder is written in <Big O notation>.

= ii
{parent=c}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}

Put $h=x-\pi/2$. The <sine> addition identity gives $\sin x=\cos h=1-h^2/2+h^4/24+O(h^6)$. Applying the <exponential series> gives
$$
\begin{aligned}
e^{\sin x}&=e\exp\left(-\frac{h^2}{2}+\frac{h^4}{24}+O(h^6)\right)\\
&=e\left[1-\frac{h^2}{2}+\left(\frac1{24}+\frac18\right)h^4+O(h^6)\right].
\end{aligned}
$$
Thus the first three nonzero terms in the <Taylor series> about $\pi/2$ are
$$
\boxed{e^{\sin x}=e-\frac e2\left(x-\frac\pi2\right)^2+\frac e6\left(x-\frac\pi2\right)^4+O\left(\left(x-\frac\pi2\right)^6\right).}
$$

