With $N/2$ white balls, uniform <sampling without replacement> gives the <probability>
$$
p_w=\frac{N/2}{N}\frac{N/2-1}{N-1}=\frac{N-2}{4(N-1)}.
$$
Rearranging, $4p_w(N-1)=N-2$, so
$$
\boxed{N=\frac{2-4p_w}{1-4p_w}=\frac{2(1-2p_w)}{1-4p_w}.}
$$
A finite admissible population has even $N\geq2$ and $0\leq p_w<1/4$, so the denominator is nonzero.

= ii
{parent=a}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}

Interchanging the two colours preserves the <probability distribution> when their population counts are equal. Thus the <probability> of two black balls is also $p_w$. The three mutually exclusive possibilities are two white, two black, or different colours, so
$$
\boxed{p_d=1-2p_w.}
$$

= b
{parent=16}
{scope}

= i
{parent=b}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=i}

The <probability> that the first ball is white is $x$. Partitioning that event according to the second colour shows that $P(WB)=x-p_w$. Uniform <sampling without replacement> makes the two draw positions exchangeable, so $P(BW)=P(WB)$. Therefore
$$
\boxed{p_d=2(x-p_w).}
$$
For a direct count, the same value is $2(xN)((1-x)N)/[N(N-1)]$.

= ii
{parent=b}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}

The <probability> of two black balls is the complement of the other two possibilities:
$$
P(BB)=1-p_w-p_d=1-2x+p_w.
$$
The <probability> of equal colours is $P(WW)+P(BB)=1-2x+2p_w$. Thus the required <conditional probability> is
$$
\boxed{P(BB\mid\text{same colour})=\frac{1-2x+p_w}{1-2x+2p_w}.}
$$
This is defined when the conditioning event has positive <probability>. If $N=2$ with one ball of each colour, that event is impossible and the <conditional probability> is undefined.

= c
{parent=16}
