{parent=c}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=i}

With equally many balls of each colour, uniform <sampling without replacement> gives
$$
p_w=\frac{N/2}{N}\frac{N/2-1}{N-1}\frac{N/2-2}{N-2}=\frac{N-4}{8(N-1)}.
$$
Here an admissible population producing positive <probability> for three white balls has even $N\geq6$, so the cancellation is legitimate. Equating this to $2/19$ gives $19(N-4)=16(N-1)$, hence
$$
\boxed{N=20.}
$$

= ii
{parent=c}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}

There are ten balls of each colour. Under uniform <sampling without replacement>, all $\binom{20}{12}$ subsets of twelve labelled balls are equally likely. A favourable subset chooses four of the ten white balls and eight of the ten black balls, giving $\binom{10}{4}\binom{10}{8}$ choices. The <hypergeometric distribution> therefore gives
$$
\boxed{P(\text{four white})=\frac{\binom{10}{4}\binom{10}{8}}{\binom{20}{12}}.}
$$
Thus the requested integers are $\boxed{N_1=N_2=10,\ m_1=4,\ m_2=8}$, with $N=20$ and $k=12$.

