The original PDF places the $-1$ outside the square root. Use the <substitution rule> with $t=\sqrt x$, so $dx=2t\,dt$ and the endpoints become $2$ and $3$. Polynomial division gives
$$
\int_2^3\frac{2t}{t-1}\,dt=2\int_2^3\left(1+\frac1{t-1}\right)dt=2[t+\ln(t-1)]_2^3.
$$
Hence the <definite integral> is
$$
\boxed{2+2\ln2.}
$$

= b
{parent=17}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=b}

Use the <substitution rule> $t=\tan x$, with $dt=(1+\tan^2x)\,dx$. The endpoints become $1$ and $\sqrt3$, so
$$
\int_1^{\sqrt3}\frac{dt}{(1+t)^2}=\left[-\frac1{1+t}\right]_1^{\sqrt3}=\frac12-\frac1{1+\sqrt3}=\boxed{1-\frac{\sqrt3}{2}}.
$$

= c
{parent=17}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=c}

Take $t=e^x>0$ in the <substitution rule>, so $dx=dt/t$. The transformed <integral> is
$$
\int\frac{t-2}{t^2+1}\,dt=\frac12\ln(t^2+1)-2\arctan t+C.
$$
The two terms follow from the <derivatives> of the <natural logarithm> and the <inverse tangent>. Returning to $x$ gives the <antiderivative>
$$
\boxed{\frac12\ln(1+e^{2x})-2\arctan(e^x)+C.}
$$

= d
{parent=17}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=d}

On an <interval> where $\tan x$ is defined, use the <substitution rule> $t=\tan x$. Then $dx=dt/(1+t^2)$ and $\cos^2x=1/(1+t^2)$, giving
$$
\int\frac{dx}{1+3\cos^2x}=\int\frac{dt}{t^2+4}=\boxed{\frac12\arctan\left(\frac{\tan x}{2}\right)+C.}
$$
The <inverse tangent> expression is valid on each <interval> $(j\pi-\pi/2,j\pi+\pi/2)$ separately. The original integrand is continuous for all real $x$, since its denominator is at least $1$. To obtain one continuous <antiderivative> on the whole real axis, add $j\pi/2$ on the $j$th such <interval> and use the continuous limits at their endpoints.

