{parent=i}

The first coordinate direction is already an <eigenvector> of $A$ with <eigenvalue> $4$. On the last two coordinates, the <matrix> is $\begin{pmatrix}2&4\\4&2\end{pmatrix}$, with <characteristic polynomial>
$$
\det\begin{pmatrix}\lambda-2&-4\\-4&\lambda-2\end{pmatrix}=(\lambda-2)^2-16=(\lambda-6)(\lambda+2).
$$
Thus the three <eigenvalues> are $4,6,-2$. For $\lambda=6$, the last two components are equal; for $\lambda=-2$, they are opposite. Normalising by the <Euclidean norm> gives the complete list
$$
\boxed{\begin{array}{c|c}
\lambda&\text{unit eigenvector}\\\hline
4&(1,0,0)^T\\
6&(0,1,1)^T/\sqrt2\\
-2&(0,1,-1)^T/\sqrt2
\end{array}}
$$
Each sign-reversed <vector> is an equally valid choice. The three displayed <eigenvectors> are mutually <orthogonal>, as expected for a real <symmetric matrix> with distinct <eigenvalues>.

= ii
{parent=a}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}

Subtracting the second row from the third gives a third row $(0,2,0)$, so expansion along it shows $\det B=-2\ne0$. Thus the <matrix inverse> exists and every right-hand side has a unique solution.

To calculate it, temporarily solve $B\mathbf x=\mathbf b$ with general $\mathbf b=(b_1,b_2,b_3)^T$. Subtracting the second equation from the third and from the first gives
$$
2x_2=b_3-b_2,\qquad x_1-x_2=b_1-b_2.
$$
Back-substitution into the second equation gives
$$
x_1=b_1-\frac32b_2+\frac12b_3,\quad x_2=-\frac12b_2+\frac12b_3,\quad x_3=-b_1+\frac72b_2-\frac32b_3.
$$
Therefore
$$
\boxed{B^{-1}=\begin{pmatrix}1&-3/2&1/2\\0&-1/2&1/2\\-1&7/2&-3/2\end{pmatrix}.}
$$
For the given right-hand side, <matrix multiplication> yields the unique answer
$$
\boxed{\mathbf x=B^{-1}(1,2,3)^T=(-1/2,1/2,3/2)^T.}
$$

= b
