= Solution
{parent=i}

The three coordinate <basis vectors> are <eigenvectors>, so $M$ is diagonal in those coordinates. Exactly one coordinate is multiplied by $-1$, while the other two are fixed. The three possibilities are
$$
\boxed{M=\operatorname{diag}(-1,1,1),\quad\operatorname{diag}(1,-1,1),\quad\operatorname{diag}(1,1,-1).}
$$
They are respectively reflections in the coordinate <planes> $x=0$, $y=0$ and $z=0$. Each is a <Householder reflection> with <unit normal> equal to the coordinate <eigenvector> carrying the <eigenvalue> $-1$.

= ii
{parent=b}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}

For each possibility in part (i), squaring the diagonal entries gives $M^2=I$. Equivalently, the <linear transformation> fixes a <vector space basis> after being applied twice. Therefore
$$
\boxed{M^2\begin{pmatrix}x\\y\\z\end{pmatrix}=\begin{pmatrix}x\\y\\z\end{pmatrix}.}
$$
Geometrically, reflecting twice in the same <plane> returns every <vector> to itself.

