Apply the <Leibniz rule> for the $n$th <derivative> of a product. Only the first three terms survive, because all <derivatives> of $x^2$ of order greater than two vanish and every <derivative> of $e^x$ is $e^x$:
$$
\frac{d^n}{dx^n}(x^2e^x)=e^x\left[\binom n0x^2+\binom n1(2x)+\binom n2(2)\right]=e^x[x^2+2nx+n(n-1)].
$$
At $n=100$ this gives
$$
\boxed{\frac{d^{100}}{dx^{100}}(x^2e^x)=e^x(x^2+200x+9900).}
$$

= b
{parent=19}
{scope}

= i
{parent=b}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=i}

The <epsilon-delta definition of continuity> says that $f$ is continuous at $a$ if
$$
\boxed{\text{for every }\varepsilon>0\text{ there exists }\delta>0\text{ such that }|x-a|<\delta\implies|f(x)-f(a)|<\varepsilon.}
$$
Here $a$ belongs to the domain of $f$, and the implication is required for every $x$ in that domain. The number $\delta$ may depend on $\varepsilon$ and $a$, but must work for all such $x$ simultaneously.

= ii
{parent=b}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}

The bound $|\cos(1/x)|\leq1$ gives $|f(x)-f(0)|\leq|x|^p$ when $x\ne0$. If $p>0$, given any $\varepsilon>0$, choose $\delta=\varepsilon^{1/p}$. Then $0<|x|<\delta$ implies
$$
|f(x)-f(0)|\leq|x|^p<\delta^p=\varepsilon;
$$
the inequality also holds at $x=0$. This proves <continuity> using the <epsilon-delta definition of continuity>.

For necessity, take the positive <sequence> $x_n=1/(2\pi n)\to0$. Then $\cos(1/x_n)=1$, so $f(x_n)=(2\pi n)^{-p}$. For $p=0$ this equals $1$; for $p<0$ it tends to $+\infty$. In neither case does it tend to $f(0)=0$, so <continuity> fails. Consequently
$$
\boxed{f\text{ is continuous at }0\iff p>0.}
$$
This is an instance of the general <continuity and differentiability thresholds for a damped oscillation>.

= iii
{parent=b}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=iii}

The <difference quotient> at zero is
$$
\frac{f(h)-f(0)}h=\frac{|h|^p}{h}\cos(1/h),\qquad h\ne0.
$$
If $p>1$, its <absolute value> is at most $|h|^{p-1}\to0$, so $f$ is <differentiable> at zero with <derivative> $0$.

To exclude $p\leq1$, use $h_n=1/(2\pi n)$ and $k_n=1/(2\pi n+\pi/2)$. The <difference quotient> equals $(2\pi n)^{1-p}$ along the first <sequence>, and $0$ along the second. For $p=1$ the first value is $1$, while for $p<1$ it is unbounded. Hence a common finite <limit> is impossible. Therefore
$$
\boxed{f\text{ is differentiable at }0\iff p>1,\qquad f'(0)=0\text{ when }p>1.}
$$

= c
{parent=19}
