= Solution
{parent=i}

For the positive terms $a_n=n^4/3^n$, the consecutive ratio is
$$
\frac{a_{n+1}}{a_n}=\frac13\left(1+\frac1n\right)^4\longrightarrow\frac13<1.
$$
The <ratio test> therefore proves that **the <series> converges absolutely**. More explicitly, for all sufficiently large $n$ the ratio is at most a fixed $q<1$, so the tail is bounded by a convergent <geometric series>.

= ii
{parent=c}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}

Compare the positive terms with those of the <harmonic series>:
$$
\frac{(n^2+2n)/(n^3+3n^2+1)}{1/n}=\frac{n^3+2n^2}{n^3+3n^2+1}\longrightarrow1.
$$
By the <limit comparison test>, the given <series> has the same convergence behaviour as $\sum1/n$, which diverges. Thus **the <series> diverges to $+\infty$**.

