The <Leibniz integral rule> combines contributions from both moving endpoints with <differentiation under the integral sign>:
$$
\boxed{\frac d{dx}\int_{a(x)}^{b(x)}f(x,t)\,dt=f(x,b(x))b'(x)-f(x,a(x))a'(x)+\int_{a(x)}^{b(x)}\frac{\partial f}{\partial x}(x,t)\,dt.}
$$
The <partial derivative> in the last <integral> holds $t$ fixed. Suitable regularity and convergence are assumed as stipulated.

= b
{parent=20}
{scope}

= i
{parent=b}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=i}

For the <Leibniz integral rule>, the upper endpoint has <derivative> $2\pi x$ and the lower endpoint has <derivative> $1$. The integrand has <partial derivative> $4xt+\sin t$. Therefore
$$
\begin{aligned}
h'(x)={}&\left(2\pi x^4+x\sin(\pi x^2)\right)2\pi x-\left(2x^3+x\sin x\right)\\
&+\int_x^{\pi x^2}(4xt+\sin t)\,dt.
\end{aligned}
$$
The last <integral> is $[2xt^2-\cos t]_x^{\pi x^2}=2\pi^2x^5-2x^3-\cos(\pi x^2)+\cos x$. Combining terms gives
$$
\boxed{h'(x)=6\pi^2x^5-4x^3+2\pi x^2\sin(\pi x^2)-x\sin x+\cos x-\cos(\pi x^2).}
$$

= ii
{parent=b}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}

Holding $x$ fixed when taking the <antiderivative> with respect to $t$ gives
$$
h(x)=[x^2t^2-x\cos t]_x^{\pi x^2}=\pi^2x^6-x^4-x\cos(\pi x^2)+x\cos x.
$$
Use the <product rule> and the <chain rule> to differentiate this explicit expression:
$$
\boxed{h'(x)=6\pi^2x^5-4x^3-\cos(\pi x^2)+2\pi x^2\sin(\pi x^2)+\cos x-x\sin x.}
$$
This agrees term by term with part (i).

= c
{parent=20}
