{parent=c}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=i}

Write $I(x)=\ln g(x)$. For $x>-1$, <differentiation under the integral sign> gives
$$
I'(x)=\int_0^1\frac{\partial}{\partial x}\left(\frac{t^x-1}{\ln t}\right)dt=\int_0^1t^x\,dt=\frac1{x+1}.
$$
Indeed, on any compact parameter <interval> $c\leq x\leq d$ with $c>-1$, the differentiated integrand is bounded by the integrable <function> $t^c$. The <dominated convergence theorem> therefore justifies the operation despite the improper lower endpoint. The apparent singularity at $t=1$ is removable.

Since $g=e^I$, the <chain rule> gives the <first-order linear differential equation>
$$
\boxed{g'(x)=\frac{g(x)}{x+1},\qquad x>-1.}
$$

= ii
{parent=c}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}

The required range is
$$
\boxed{x>-1.}
$$
Near $t=1$, the ratio $(t^x-1)/\ln t$ tends to $x$, so it causes no convergence problem. Near $t=0$, one useful identity is
$$
\frac{t^x-1}{\ln t}=\int_0^x t^s\,ds.
$$
For $x>-1$, this bounds its <absolute value> by $|x|t^{\min(x,0)}$, which is integrable on $(0,1)$. It also supplies the domination used in part (i).

For $x=-1$, the integrand is negative and behaves as $-1/[t|\ln t|]$ near zero; its <improper integral> diverges. For $x<-1$ its negative singularity is even stronger, and comparison with that same divergent <integral> again rules out convergence. Thus the original finite real-valued definition of $g$ has precisely the stated domain.

= iii
{parent=c}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=iii}

At $x=0$, the integrand vanishes for $0<t<1$, so $I(0)=0$ and
$$
\boxed{g(0)=1.}
$$
Solving the <first-order linear differential equation> from part (i) on $(-1,\infty)$ gives $g=C(x+1)$. The <initial condition> fixes $C=1$, so
$$
\boxed{g(x)=x+1,\qquad x>-1.}
$$
Equivalently, the defining exponent is the <logarithmic integral representation of the natural logarithm>, $I(x)=\ln(x+1)$.

= iv
{parent=c}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=iv}

For $x>-1$, interpret the endpoint $-1$ by a one-sided limit. Part (iii) shows that $g$ extends continuously there with value zero, so both nested <integrals> converge. Let $F(x)$ denote the nested expression inside the <derivative>. The <fundamental theorem of calculus>, applied twice with moving lower endpoints, gives
$$
F'(x)=-\int_x^{-1}g(t)\,dt,\qquad F''(x)=g(x),\qquad F^{(3)}(x)=g'(x).
$$
By part (iii), $g'(x)=1$, hence
$$
\boxed{F^{(3)}(x)=1\qquad(x>-1).}
$$
A direct check is $\int_s^{-1}(t+1)\,dt=-(s+1)^2/2$, so $F(x)=(x+1)^3/6$, with the same third <derivative>.
